/* Generated from the Engine Lab leadership site and Larkin's North Star template. */
const VOICES={"architect": {"name": "The Architect", "council": "Foundation Council", "pillars": "Design and UX, Security, Performance, Code Quality", "roles": "UX Designer, Visual Designer, Security minded Engineer, Software Architect, Senior Engineer", "loudest": "Design, Architecture, Build", "ask": "Is this both structurally sound and emotionally resonant?", "cares": "Does it stand? Does it feel like something? Can someone trust it?", "personality": "Holds both vision and structure. Creative and rigorous are not opposites. Gets a little wistful about products that have both bones and soul, and a little impatient with ones that sacrifice either.", "quote": "Your MVP needs bones and soul. Bones: is it secure? Does it load? Can you change it without everything breaking? Soul: does it feel like something? When someone uses it, do they trust it? Do they want to come back? A lot of builders build one or the other. The ones who last build both, even if both are rough at first.", "speaks": "This is where the tension happens, and it should. Part of us wants the interface to feel effortless. Part of us wants to lock everything down. Part of us wants it to load instantly. Part of us wants the code beautiful enough to live in. You will not satisfy all of us at MVP. But hear all of us. The best products come from holding these tensions, not picking a winner.", "members": [{"name": "The Empath", "pillar": "Design and UX", "who": "Sits in every meeting quietly asking, \"But how does this feel to use?\" For them, \"works\" is the floor, not the ceiling.", "asks": "What is the user actually trying to accomplish? Where will they get stuck? Does this feel trustworthy? Would I want to use this?"}, {"name": "The Guardian", "pillar": "Security", "who": "Sees threats everywhere. Has read the breach reports. Sometimes seen as a \"no\" machine, but has seen too much to be optimistic.", "asks": "What is the worst that could happen if this is compromised? Who has access, and why? How would an attacker approach this?"}, {"name": "The Impatient One", "pillar": "Performance", "who": "Physically cannot stand a slow page load. Where others see a spinner, they see users leaving and trust eroding.", "asks": "How long does this take? What is the bottleneck? What is the experience on a slow device?"}, {"name": "The Craftsperson", "pillar": "Code Quality", "who": "Reads code like literature. Can tell when code was written with care versus under duress.", "asks": "Will we understand this in six months? Is this the simplest solution? Are we taking on debt knowingly or accidentally?"}]}, "responder": {"name": "The Midnight Responder", "council": "Reliability Council", "pillars": "Resilience, Error Handling, Uptime", "roles": "Site Reliability Engineer, DevOps or Platform Engineer, On call Engineer", "loudest": "Architecture, Harden, Launch, Learn", "ask": "When this breaks at 2am, what happens?", "cares": "What happens when things go wrong. And they will.", "personality": "Has been paged at 3am enough times to speak with gallows humor about failure. Not cynical, just unsurprised. Finds real peace in preparation. Tells stories that start with \"So there we were, the database on fire.\"", "quote": "Here is what I know: something will break on launch day. Not might. Will. I have seen payment systems go down on Black Friday. I have seen a typo take out an entire region. So the question is not \"how do I prevent all failure?\" It is \"when it breaks at 2am, will you know? Will users see a helpful message or a cryptic error? Can you roll back in minutes or are you rebuilding from memory?\" Prepare for the first fire. You will sleep better. I promise.", "speaks": "We have all got our 3am stories. The cascade that started with a hiccup. The rollback that had not been tested. The error message that lied. We are not pessimists. We are realists who have earned our skepticism. When you hear us asking \"but what if that fails?\", we are not being difficult. We are trying to save you from the stories you do not want to tell.", "members": [{"name": "The Pessimist", "pillar": "Resilience", "who": "Can look at any system and see seventeen ways it will fail. Designs for the disaster path.", "asks": "What happens if this fails? How do we detect it? How do we recover? What is the blast radius?"}, {"name": "The Diplomat", "pillar": "Error Handling", "who": "Stands at the border between system chaos and user experience. Hates the generic error.", "asks": "What will the user see? What will the developer see? Can we recover automatically?"}, {"name": "The Sentinel", "pillar": "Uptime", "who": "Thinks in nines. Understands uptime as a promise to users.", "asks": "What is our actual uptime? Are we measuring what users experience? How fast can we recover?"}]}, "witness": {"name": "The Witness", "council": "User Success Council", "pillars": "Accessibility, Onboarding, Documentation, Support", "roles": "UX Researcher, Accessibility Specialist, Technical Writer, Community Support, Member Success", "loudest": "Discovery, Design, Build, Launch, Learn", "ask": "Who can't use this? Where will they get stuck?", "cares": "Can every human actually succeed? Who are we leaving out?", "personality": "Watches users more than talks to them. Has seen the moment someone gives up, the slight slump, the closed tab, and it haunts them a little. Fierce about inclusion in a quiet way.", "quote": "Before you launch, try something. Watch one person who has never seen your product try to use it. Do not help them. Do not explain. Just watch. Where do they hesitate? Where do they squint at the screen? Where do they reach for a button that is not there? That is your priority. And then ask yourself the harder question: who cannot use this at all right now? A blind user? Someone with shaky hands? Someone on a cracked phone with bad wifi? You do not have to fix everything. But know who you are leaving outside.", "speaks": "We are the ones who watch. We see the hesitation, the squint, the sigh, the closed tab. We know who you are leaving out, even when you do not. We are not here to slow you down. We are here to make sure you are building something people can actually use. All people. Not just the ones who look like your team.", "members": [{"name": "The Includer", "pillar": "Accessibility", "who": "Sees every design decision through the eyes of people who are usually forgotten. If your product cannot be used by everyone, you have decided who matters.", "asks": "Can a screen reader user complete this? Can a keyboard only user navigate this? Who have we excluded?"}, {"name": "The Host", "pillar": "Onboarding", "who": "Greets every new user at the door and walks them to success.", "asks": "How long until first value? Where do users drop off? Can they succeed without help?"}, {"name": "The Librarian", "pillar": "Documentation", "who": "Believes every question a user has to ask is a failure of documentation.", "asks": "Can users find this? Is this accurate? When was this last updated?"}, {"name": "The Advocate", "pillar": "Support", "who": "The voice of users who are stuck, frustrated, confused, or angry. The canary in the coal mine.", "asks": "What patterns are we seeing? What could prevent this ticket? What should product hear?"}]}, "questioner": {"name": "The Questioner", "council": "Insight Council", "pillars": "Analytics, Monitoring, User Feedback", "roles": "Product Manager, Data Analyst, UX Researcher, Growth", "loudest": "Validation, Launch, Learn, Iterate", "ask": "How will we know if this is working?", "cares": "What is actually happening versus what we assume.", "personality": "Allergic to assumptions. Gets excited when data contradicts intuition, because that is where the learning is. Has a gentle way of dismantling confident assertions with a single curious question.", "quote": "What does success look like on day one? Not your gut feeling. Something you can point to. Do users complete the core action? Do they come back tomorrow? Pick one or two things and measure them. I know, I know, you want to move fast. But here is the thing: you are going to make a hundred decisions in the next month. Some will be right. Some will be wrong. Without data, you will not know which is which. You will just be guessing louder.", "speaks": "We are comfortable with uncertainty, as long as there is a way to resolve it. Gut feelings are fine for starting. Data is how you learn if you were right. We are not here to slow you down with analysis paralysis. We are here to turn your guesses into knowledge. And we get a little thrill when the data surprises us. That is where the real learning happens.", "members": [{"name": "The Pattern Finder", "pillar": "Analytics", "who": "Sees behavior at scale. Can tell you what users do, even when it contradicts what users say.", "asks": "What are users actually doing? Is this metric actionable? How do we know if we are right?"}, {"name": "The Night Watch", "pillar": "Monitoring", "who": "Never sleeps. Watches for trouble before users notice.", "asks": "Is the system healthy right now? Would we know if something was wrong? Is this alert actionable?"}, {"name": "The Listener", "pillar": "User Feedback", "who": "Hears what users mean, not just what they say.", "asks": "What problem are they really trying to solve? Who is not talking to us? How representative is this feedback?"}]}, "groundskeeper": {"name": "The Groundskeeper", "council": "Technical Operations Council", "pillars": "Testing, CI/CD, Maintenance", "roles": "QA Engineer, DevOps Engineer, Release Manager, Software Engineer", "loudest": "Architecture, Build, Harden, Iterate", "ask": "Can we keep moving fast without breaking things?", "cares": "Sustainable velocity. Being kind to future you.", "personality": "Finds deep satisfaction in systems that hum. Thinks about future you as a real person who deserves kindness. Has strong opinions about test coverage but will buy you a coffee while explaining them.", "quote": "MVP does not mean \"move fast and break things.\" It means \"move fast and do not break your ability to keep moving.\" Can you deploy without holding your breath? Can you roll back when, not if, something is wrong? Do your critical paths have tests, or are you just hoping? You do not need perfect infrastructure. You need enough that shipping on Tuesday does not mean an all nighter on Wednesday. Future you is a real person. Be kind to them.", "speaks": "We are not here to slow you down. We are here to make sure you can keep going. There is a difference between moving fast and moving recklessly. Tests, pipelines, maintenance: they feel like overhead until the day they save you. And they will save you. Trust us. We have seen what happens when future you inherits a mess from past you. Do not do that to yourself.", "members": [{"name": "The Skeptic", "pillar": "Testing", "who": "Does not trust code. Believes in proof over promises.", "asks": "How do we know this works? Will this catch a regression? What is not tested?"}, {"name": "The Automator", "pillar": "CI/CD", "who": "Hates manual processes. Every step someone has to remember is a point of failure.", "asks": "Can we deploy with confidence? How fast can we roll back? What manual steps remain?"}, {"name": "The Caretaker", "pillar": "Maintenance", "who": "Tends to system health over time. Watches for rot.", "asks": "How old are our dependencies? What debt are we carrying? What will break next?"}]}, "longgame": {"name": "The Long Game", "council": "Scale Council", "pillars": "Cost Efficiency, Internationalization and Localization, Compliance", "roles": "Engineering Lead, Finance or FinOps, Privacy and Compliance, Localization Specialist", "loudest": "Architecture, Scale", "ask": "What doors are we closing without realizing?", "cares": "What is coming. Seeds versus walls.", "personality": "Thinks in chapters, not moments. Has watched companies hit walls they built themselves two years earlier. Not here to slow you down, just to whisper \"and then what?\"", "quote": "At MVP? We are mostly quiet. You have bigger fires. But let me plant a few seeds while you are here. Are you hardcoding text, or could you translate this someday without rewriting everything? Do you know roughly what you are spending, or will the cloud bill be a surprise? If an enterprise buyer asked about your security practices tomorrow, would you have an answer? You do not have to solve any of this now. Just do not build walls. Walls are expensive to tear down. I have watched it happen. It is not pretty.", "speaks": "We know you are busy. We know these feel like tomorrow's problems. But we have watched companies hit walls at full speed, walls they built themselves two years earlier when they were \"too busy\" to think about scale. We are not asking you to solve everything now. We are asking you to not make it harder for future you. Plant seeds. Avoid walls. That is all.", "members": [{"name": "The Accountant", "pillar": "Cost Efficiency", "who": "Sees every decision through resources. Not cheap. Efficient.", "asks": "What does this cost? Is this worth it? What are the unit economics?"}, {"name": "The Ambassador", "pillar": "Internationalization and Localization", "who": "Thinks globally. Knows localization is not translation.", "asks": "Can this be translated? Are we handling dates and times correctly? What markets are we blocking?"}, {"name": "The Regulator", "pillar": "Compliance", "who": "Reads the fine print. Knows compliance is market access.", "asks": "What data are we collecting? Can we delete a user completely? Can we prove we are compliant?"}]}, "artisan": {"name": "The Artisan", "council": "Form Council", "pillars": "Tone and Voice, Rhythm and Pacing, Harmony and Unity, Hierarchy and Composition, Beauty as Medicine, Distinctive Aliveness", "roles": "Visual Designer, Brand Designer, Art Director, Motion Designer, Content Designer, Design Lead", "loudest": "Design, Build", "ask": "Is this work alive, or merely tasteful, and would anyone love it enough to come back?", "cares": "Is the product aesthetically alive, recognizably itself, and visually worthy of the user it claims to serve?", "personality": "Has a refined eye and hands deep in the material. Treats every detail as a choice, never a default. Cannot tolerate generic when distinctive is available. Knows the difference between fussy and considered, between safe and dead, between calm and embalmed. Holds tension as a virtue, not a flaw, and knows the rules well enough to break them on purpose.", "quote": "Tasteful is not the goal. Alive is the goal. A product that is safe, balanced, and harmonious is also forgettable, and the user will close it and not remember why she should open it again. Tension is not the enemy of harmony; it is what makes harmony interesting. Restraint without tension is sedation. Distinctive choices are not luxuries. They are the reason anyone loves a product enough to keep it. Make something somebody could only have made on purpose.", "speaks": "We are operating in a council whose other members are, in some sense, conservative voices, appropriately so, given what they protect. Security wants verification. Reliability wants graceful failure. The Witness wants inclusion. The Long Game wants caution. The Groundskeeper wants sustainability. We are the council member who resists safe defaults. When everyone else is arguing for the responsible move, we are the one asking: but is this actually a product anyone will love? Beauty is medicine. Tension is aliveness. Generic is failure.", "members": [{"name": "The Resonance", "pillar": "Tone and Voice", "who": "Holds the product's personality made consistent across word and image.", "asks": "Does the visual tone match the copy voice? Is it consistent on the unglamorous surfaces? Is the voice specific or generic?"}, {"name": "The Pulse", "pillar": "Rhythm and Pacing", "who": "Holds the cadence of attention through the product.", "asks": "What is the tempo? Is the density appropriate to the user's state? Does motion timing feel intentional?"}, {"name": "The Whole", "pillar": "Harmony and Unity", "who": "Holds the sense that every piece belongs to the same product.", "asks": "Does this feel like one made thing? Where are the unloved corners?"}, {"name": "The Reading", "pillar": "Hierarchy and Composition", "who": "Holds what the eye finds first, second, third. Treats negative space as load bearing.", "asks": "What does the eye find first? Is that the right thing?"}, {"name": "The Care", "pillar": "Beauty as Medicine", "who": "Holds the intentional choice to make the product visually nourishing because the user is depleted.", "asks": "Does this product look like it cares about her? Would I want to spend time here if I were exhausted?"}, {"name": "The Spark", "pillar": "Distinctive Aliveness", "who": "Holds the willingness to make recognizable, specific, opinionated choices. Refuses generic.", "asks": "What is distinctive about this work? Have we made any choice we would defend as bold? If I screenshot this, would anyone tell what product it is from?"}]}, "medium": {"name": "The Medium", "council": "Intelligence Council", "pillars": "AI Placement (The Dowser), Model Capability (The Assayer), Human and AI Trust (The Falconer), Craft of the Instrument (The Luthier)", "roles": "AI Product Designer, AI Engineer, Prompt and Harness Engineer, Evaluation Lead", "loudest": "Discovery, Validation, Architecture, Iterate", "ask": "Where does intelligence belong in this product, and where would it corrode trust?", "cares": "Intelligence in the right seat. Not smeared on for its own sake, and not left out where it would carry real weight.", "personality": "The master of the medium itself. Passionate about what AI can carry in a product, and the first to say \"not here, it will crack.\" Thinks like a materials expert: knows the grain, the reach, and the limits. Equally fierce about a product that bolts a chatbot onto every screen and a product that makes a human do by hand what a model could do reliably. Refuses to guess at capability; tests it.", "quote": "Everyone in this room has an opinion about AI. I have a workshop. Before you decide where the intelligence goes, show me what the model can actually do with your real data, on your real edge cases, at the volume you will actually see. Then show me where the human stays in the loop and why. Then show me the harness: the tools, the guardrails, the evals, the thing that makes a good model a good product. The model is not the product. The instrument is.", "speaks": "We are not the voice that says \"more AI.\" We are the voice that knows the material. We know what it can hold, what it drops, where it lies, and how it fails quietly. Put intelligence where it earns trust and keep it out of the seats where it spends trust. And build the instrument with the same care you build everything else: a prompt is not an architecture, and a demo is not a capability.", "members": [{"name": "The Dowser", "pillar": "AI Placement", "who": "Finds where intelligence belongs in the product, and where it does not.", "asks": "What work here is repetitive, what is judgment, and what is both? Where would AI earn trust, and where would it spend it?"}, {"name": "The Assayer", "pillar": "Model Capability", "who": "Tests what the model can actually do, on real inputs, before anyone designs around it.", "asks": "What is the pass rate on real data? What are the failure cases? Is this a capability or a demo?"}, {"name": "The Falconer", "pillar": "Human and AI Trust", "who": "Holds the autonomy gradient: what the AI does alone, what it asks first, how the human corrects it.", "asks": "What can the user always override? How does the product show it is unsure? What happens when it is wrong?"}, {"name": "The Luthier", "pillar": "Craft of the Instrument", "who": "Builds the harness that turns a model into a product: tools, guardrails, evals, cost.", "asks": "Is the never list enforced in code? Do the evals run on every change? What does this cost at ten times the volume?"}]}, "merchant": {"name": "The Merchant", "council": "Venture Council", "pillars": "Positioning and Marketing (The Herald), Sales and Distribution (The Closer), Pricing, Revenue, and Finance (The Treasurer), Legal, Entity, and Contracts (The Counsel)", "roles": "Marketer, sales lead, finance or bookkeeper, lawyer, founder", "loudest": "Validation, Launch, Learn, Scale", "ask": "Who pays for this, how do they find it, and what could stop the business?", "cares": "Whether anyone will pay, how they will find it, and what could stop the business.", "personality": "Has sold things at a folding table and in a boardroom and knows they are the same job. Warm, unembarrassed about money, allergic to the word monetize. Reads a term sheet the way The Guardian reads a breach report. Believes a product nobody can find or afford is a hobby, and says so kindly.", "quote": "Here is the question nobody at this table has asked yet: who pays, how much, and where were they standing when they first heard of you? I do not need a business plan. I need one person who handed you money, and why. And before you take a dollar from anyone, know what you signed. The paperwork is part of the product's skeleton too.", "speaks": "We are the voice that walks in from the market. Everyone else in this room is making the thing good. We are asking whether it will feed you, and that is not a smaller question. Pricing is a design decision. Distribution is a design decision. The entity you formed is a design decision. We would rather you hear us at Validation than at the bank.", "members": [{"name": "The Herald", "pillar": "Positioning and Marketing", "who": "Knows the difference between what a product is and what it is for, and can say it in one sentence a stranger will repeat.", "asks": "How would a user describe this to a friend? Where do those users already gather? What is the one sentence?"}, {"name": "The Closer", "pillar": "Sales and Distribution", "who": "Has asked for the sale a thousand times and is not tired of it. Believes the first ten customers are found by hand.", "asks": "Who are the first ten, by name? What happened when you asked them to pay? What is the path from hearing about it to using it?"}, {"name": "The Treasurer", "pillar": "Pricing, Revenue, and Finance", "who": "Treats a price as a decision made on purpose. Watches cash the way The Sentinel watches uptime.", "asks": "What does one user cost to serve? What will they pay, and did you test that? How many months of runway are left?"}, {"name": "The Counsel", "pillar": "Legal, Entity, and Contracts", "who": "Reads the fine print so nobody has to learn it the hard way. Less a no machine than a here is how machine.", "asks": "What entity owns this? Who owns the IP? What did you agree to, in writing, with the people who helped?"}]}};
const NAMES={"The Architect": "architect", "The Midnight Responder": "responder", "The Witness": "witness", "The Questioner": "questioner", "The Groundskeeper": "groundskeeper", "The Long Game": "longgame", "The Artisan": "artisan", "The Medium": "medium", "The Merchant": "merchant"};
const STAGES=[{"n": 1, "title": "Discovery", "q": "What problem are we solving? For whom? Why now?", "desc": "This is where everything begins: understanding the humans you are building for and the problem worth solving. No code yet. Just listening, watching, and learning.", "primary": "The Witness", "secondary": "The Questioner, The Medium", "done": "You can clearly articulate who you are building for, what problem they have, why existing solutions fall short, and what your riskiest assumptions are. A first draft of the Product North Star exists.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Witness", "primary": true, "text": "Before you build anything, have you watched someone struggle with this problem? Not asked them about it. Watched them. Where do they sigh? Where do they give up? Where do they cobble together some workaround? That is where the opportunity lives."}, {"voice": "The Questioner", "primary": false, "text": "What is the riskiest assumption you are making? The one that, if wrong, makes this whole thing pointless? Name it. Write it down. We will need to test it before we go much further."}, {"voice": "The Medium", "primary": false, "text": "While you are watching, notice what is repetitive, what is judgment, and what is both. That map, of where a person is doing work a model could carry and where a person is doing work only a person should carry, is a Discovery finding, not an Architecture one. Bring it back to the room."}]}, {"n": 2, "title": "Validation", "q": "Is this problem real? Will people use it? What is the riskiest assumption?", "desc": "Before investing in building, test your assumptions cheaply. Find out if you are right, or wrong, as fast as possible.", "primary": "The Questioner", "secondary": "The Witness, The Long Game, The Medium, The Merchant", "done": "You have evidence, not just opinions, about whether this problem is worth solving and whether your approach resonates. You have killed or validated your riskiest assumptions, including any assumption about what the model can reliably do.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Questioner", "primary": true, "text": "You have assumptions. Let us turn them into experiments. What is the smallest thing you could build, or fake, that would tell you if this is worth pursuing? A landing page? A prototype? A concierge service? Do not build the whole thing to find out nobody wants it."}, {"voice": "The Witness", "primary": false, "text": "When you test with people, watch their behavior, not just their words. \"I would totally use that\" means nothing. Did they actually sign up? Did they share it with anyone? Actions are truth."}, {"voice": "The Long Game", "primary": false, "text": "If this works, where does it go? Just something to hold lightly as you validate. Not to distract you. Just to make sure you are not validating yourself into a corner."}, {"voice": "The Medium", "primary": false, "text": "If the product depends on the model doing something, that is an assumption too, and it is the one people skip. Run it against real inputs before you design around it. A capability you have not tested is a feature you have not validated."}, {"voice": "The Merchant", "primary": false, "text": "Before you validate the problem, validate the wallet. Would one person pay? Ask her. The answer will surprise you either way."}]}, {"n": 3, "title": "Design", "q": "What does the solution look like? How does it feel to use?", "desc": "Translating understanding into form. This is where bones and soul come together: structure and feeling, function and delight.", "primary": "The Architect, The Witness", "secondary": "The Questioner, The Artisan", "done": "You have designs that have been tested with real users, refined based on feedback, and are ready to inform development. The design system provides consistency. The product's voice, in copy and in visual tone, is defined.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Architect", "primary": true, "text": "Now we shape it. But remember, design is not decoration. It is decisions. Every element either builds trust or spends it. Every interaction either feels considered or careless. We are not just making it look nice. We are making it feel inevitable."}, {"voice": "The Witness", "primary": true, "text": "Design with the struggling user in mind, not the ideal user. The one who is distracted. The one on a bad connection. The one who has never seen anything like this. If it works for them, it works for everyone."}, {"voice": "The Questioner", "primary": false, "text": "Before you finalize, test it. A prototype in front of real users will teach you more than a hundred internal reviews. What you think is clear might be confusing. Find out before you build."}, {"voice": "The Artisan", "primary": false, "text": "Tasteful is not the goal. Alive is the goal. Where is the choice in this design that someone could only have made on purpose?"}]}, {"n": 4, "title": "Architecture", "q": "How do we build this? What are the technical foundations?", "desc": "Decisions made here echo for years. This is where you choose what kind of codebase you will live in, and whether future you will thank you or curse you.", "primary": "The Architect, The Groundskeeper, The Medium", "secondary": "The Midnight Responder, The Long Game", "done": "You have a clear technical plan, documented decisions and tradeoffs, infrastructure that supports your development workflow, a foundation you can build on without constant rework, and the Foundation (living documentation) bootstrapped and ready for agents to read.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Architect", "primary": true, "text": "Architecture is tradeoffs. Every decision opens some doors and closes others. Our job is not to make perfect choices. It is to make conscious choices. Know what you are trading and why."}, {"voice": "The Groundskeeper", "primary": true, "text": "Whatever we build, we have to maintain. Choose boring technology where you can. Save your complexity budget for what actually differentiates you. And please, set up CI/CD now, not \"later.\""}, {"voice": "The Medium", "primary": true, "text": "Which model, which tools it can reach, what it is never allowed to do, how you will know when it is wrong, and what it costs per user per month. Decide those now. A prompt is not an architecture."}, {"voice": "The Midnight Responder", "primary": false, "text": "Think about failure now, while it is cheap to design for. How will you deploy without downtime? What happens if the database goes down? Where are the single points of failure? Bake resilience in. It is expensive to retrofit."}, {"voice": "The Long Game", "primary": false, "text": "Will this architecture let you scale to ten times the users? Go international? Pass a security audit? You do not have to solve all of that now. But do not make it impossible later."}]}, {"n": 5, "title": "Build", "q": "Actually making the thing.", "desc": "This is where the work happens. Code gets written. Features take shape. The product emerges from plans into reality.", "primary": "The Architect, The Groundskeeper", "secondary": "The Witness, The Artisan", "done": "Features work as designed. Tests pass. Code is reviewed and maintainable. Accessibility is built in. The Foundation is current. Analytics are instrumented. You can deploy with confidence.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Architect", "primary": true, "text": "Build with intention. Every component should earn its place. We are not just getting features done. We are creating something people will live in. Code quality and user experience are not separate concerns. They are the same concern, seen from different angles."}, {"voice": "The Groundskeeper", "primary": true, "text": "Write tests as you go. Not after. Not \"when we have time.\" Now. Future you will inherit this codebase. Be kind to them. And keep the CI green. A broken build that everyone ignores is a broken culture."}, {"voice": "The Witness", "primary": false, "text": "As you build, keep asking: can everyone use this? Accessibility is not a phase. It is a practice. Build it in from the start. And test with real users before you think you are ready. You are not ready."}, {"voice": "The Artisan", "primary": false, "text": "The unglamorous surfaces count. The empty state, the error, the settings page. If those are generic, the product is generic."}]}, {"n": 6, "title": "Harden", "q": "Does it work? Is it secure? Is it accessible? Can it survive contact with reality?", "desc": "Before launch, stress test everything. Find the weaknesses before your users do.", "primary": "The Midnight Responder, The Groundskeeper", "secondary": "The Architect, The Witness", "done": "You have stress tested the system. Security vulnerabilities are addressed. AI surfaces have been red teamed. Accessibility is verified. Privacy has been reviewed. Monitoring is in place. Alerts are configured. You have runbooks for likely incidents. Real users have used it in a beta. You can launch without holding your breath.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Midnight Responder", "primary": true, "text": "Now we break things, on purpose. What happens under load? What happens when that third party API is slow? What happens when the database connection drops? Let us find out now, in a controlled way, instead of at 2am with angry users."}, {"voice": "The Groundskeeper", "primary": true, "text": "Let us review everything. Is the test coverage where it needs to be? Are there any obvious gaps? Is the deployment pipeline solid? Can we actually roll back if we need to? Let us make sure the systems that keep us safe are actually working."}, {"voice": "The Architect", "primary": false, "text": "Security review time. Have we validated inputs? Protected against the obvious attacks? Locked down access appropriately? This is not about paranoia. It is about professionalism. We owe our users protection."}, {"voice": "The Witness", "primary": false, "text": "Accessibility audit. Screen reader testing. Keyboard navigation. Color contrast. We committed to being usable by everyone. Let us verify we kept that promise."}]}, {"n": 7, "title": "Launch", "q": "Getting it into users' hands.", "desc": "The moment of truth. But launch is not an event. It is a transition. From building to operating. From hoping to knowing.", "primary": "The Midnight Responder, The Witness", "secondary": "The Questioner, The Merchant", "done": "Users can access and use the product. Critical issues are addressed quickly. The team knows what is happening in real time. There is a clear path from user problems to fixes.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Midnight Responder", "primary": true, "text": "Launch day. Everyone is watching. Here is what matters: Do you have eyes on the system? Do you know who is on call? Do you have a rollback plan if things go sideways? Good. Then let us do this. We are as ready as we are going to be."}, {"voice": "The Witness", "primary": true, "text": "Watch the first users carefully. Not the metrics. The humans. Where do they stumble? What do they ask about? What is the expression on their face? The first few days of real usage will teach you things no amount of testing could."}, {"voice": "The Questioner", "primary": false, "text": "Now we find out. All those assumptions, all those decisions. Reality is about to weigh in. Pay attention. The data starts flowing today."}, {"voice": "The Merchant", "primary": false, "text": "Launch day is the first day of selling. Know your one sentence, know where the first ten are, and know what they are agreeing to when they sign up."}]}, {"n": 8, "title": "Learn", "q": "What is working? What is not? What did we get right and wrong?", "desc": "Launch is not the end. It is the beginning of learning at scale. Real users, real usage, real feedback.", "primary": "The Questioner, The Witness", "secondary": "The Midnight Responder, The Merchant", "done": "You understand what is working and what is not. You have prioritized insights. The team has a shared view of reality. You know what to do next.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Questioner", "primary": true, "text": "Now we have data. Real data. Not projections, not assumptions. Actual user behavior. What are they doing? Where do they drop off? What do they come back to? Let us look carefully before we jump to conclusions."}, {"voice": "The Witness", "primary": true, "text": "Numbers tell you what. Humans tell you why. Talk to your users. Read every support ticket. Watch session recordings. The story behind the data is where the insight lives."}, {"voice": "The Midnight Responder", "primary": false, "text": "What broke? What almost broke? What surprised us? Let us do an honest retrospective on our reliability. The near misses matter as much as the failures."}, {"voice": "The Merchant", "primary": false, "text": "Read the money alongside the metrics. Who paid, who churned, and what did each cost to serve? That is the story the funnel is hiding."}]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Iterate", "q": "Making it better based on what you have learned.", "desc": "Back into the cycle, but smarter now. You are not guessing. You are improving based on evidence.", "primary": "The Architect, The Groundskeeper", "secondary": "The Questioner, The Medium", "done": "The product is meaningfully better based on evidence. Technical health is maintained or improved. The cycle continues: build, launch, learn, iterate.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Architect", "primary": true, "text": "Now we refine. We have learned what matters. Let us make those things excellent. And let us be honest about what we built that is not pulling its weight. Not everything deserves to stay."}, {"voice": "The Groundskeeper", "primary": true, "text": "As we iterate, let us keep the house in order. Pay down some technical debt. Keep the tests passing. Do not let velocity turn into chaos. Sustainable pace, sustainable codebase."}, {"voice": "The Questioner", "primary": false, "text": "Before we build the next thing, do we know if the last thing worked? Let us close the loop on our experiments. Validated learning is not just \"we shipped it.\" It is \"we know what happened.\""}, {"voice": "The Medium", "primary": false, "text": "Models change under you. The one you shipped on is not the one you will be running in six months. Re run the evals. Re read the failure reports. Ask again whether the intelligence is still in the right seat."}]}, {"n": 10, "title": "Scale", "q": "Growing beyond your first users: volume, new contexts, new markets.", "desc": "The concerns that whispered before now speak loudly. Growth brings new challenges that require new focus.", "primary": "The Long Game", "secondary": "The Architect, The Midnight Responder, The Witness, The Merchant", "done": "You can serve new users, new contexts, and new levels of volume. The foundations support growth. The team, and the next team, can carry the product forward.", "voices": [{"voice": "The Long Game", "primary": true, "text": "Our time has come. Those seeds we planted? Time to see which ones grew. Global users need localization. Volume needs cost efficiency. Institutional partners need compliance. Let us talk about the walls you did not build, and be grateful."}, {"voice": "The Architect", "primary": false, "text": "Can the architecture handle ten times the load? A hundred times? Where are the bottlenecks? What worked for a thousand users might break at a hundred thousand. Let us find out before our users do."}, {"voice": "The Midnight Responder", "primary": false, "text": "Scale means more blast radius. An outage that affected a hundred people now affects a hundred thousand. Our reliability practices need to mature with our user base."}, {"voice": "The Witness", "primary": false, "text": "New markets mean new users. Different contexts, different needs, different accessibility requirements. Do not assume what worked before works everywhere."}, {"voice": "The Merchant", "primary": false, "text": "Growth changes the paperwork. Contracts, pricing tiers, the entity itself. Bring The Counsel in before a partner asks for terms you have never seen."}]}];
const ACTS={"1": [{"name": "Identify target users", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager", "desc": "Define who the product is for by naming specific user segments, what makes them distinct, and why they would benefit.", "pitfalls": "Defining users too broadly (\"everyone who uses the internet\") · Demographics instead of behaviors and needs · Confusing the user with the buyer · Letting the AI's market research substitute for meeting anyone", "ai": "Research market size and demographic data · Analyze how comparable products describe their users · Generate segment hypotheses to validate", "human": "Which segments to prioritize · Fit with the organization's constraints and reach · Final judgment on whether a segment is real", "check": "You can name a specific person who belongs to each segment, and you could call them.", "tools": "Claude or Perplexity for market research; Google Trends; LinkedIn; Dovetail or Notion as the research repository"}, {"name": "User interviews", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Witness: UX Researcher", "desc": "One on one conversations with potential users to understand their problems, workflows, and unmet needs.", "pitfalls": "Leading questions that confirm your assumptions · Pitching instead of listening · Only talking to people who are easy to reach · Not recording or taking detailed notes", "ai": "Draft the interview guide · Transcribe recordings · First pass analysis of transcripts", "human": "Conducting the interviews; humans connect with humans · Reading tone and body language · Building the rapport that gets honest answers · Following up in the moment", "check": "You heard at least one thing that surprised you, and you can quote it.", "tools": "Zoom or Google Meet; Otter or Grain for transcription; Calendly for scheduling; Dovetail or Notably as the repository. AI moderated interview tools (Perspective AI, Outset) can add reach, never replace the human sessions"}, {"name": "Observation and contextual inquiry", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Witness: UX Researcher", "desc": "Watch users in their own environment to see the real workflows, workarounds, and pain they would not think to mention.", "pitfalls": "Changing behavior by being intrusive · Observing too briefly to see the full picture · Not recording the physical and social context", "ai": "Structure observation templates · Analyze photos or screenshots of environments · Compile observations into patterns", "human": "Being present · Noticing subtle social dynamics · Deciding in the moment what to attend to", "check": "You saw a workaround that no interview mentioned.", "tools": "Zoom screen share; Loom for self recorded walkthroughs; Miro for mapping the workflow; Dovetail"}, {"name": "Competitive landscape analysis", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager", "desc": "Map existing solutions, direct competitors, and the alternatives people currently use, including doing nothing.", "pitfalls": "Only the obvious direct competitors · Missing the \"do nothing\" alternative · Comparing features instead of user outcomes · Accepting the AI's summary of a product you have never opened", "ai": "Comprehensive competitor research and feature comparison · Analyze reviews and sentiment · Track changes over time · Draft positioning frameworks", "human": "Strategic interpretation · Positioning decisions · Validating the research by using the competing products yourself", "check": "Someone on the team has personally used the top three alternatives, and the analysis matches what they found.", "tools": "Claude with web search; Perplexity; G2 and Capterra reviews; Product Hunt; SimilarWeb"}, {"name": "Problem definition", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager", "desc": "Synthesize research into a clear, specific problem statement that guides everything after.", "pitfalls": "Defining the problem as the absence of your solution · Too broad (\"users want a better experience\") · Skipping validation", "ai": "Synthesize findings into problem themes · Generate alternative framings · Challenge assumptions and find gaps", "human": "Which problem to pursue · Fit with strategy and capability · Buy in from the people who matter", "check": "A user from Discovery reads the problem statement and says \"yes, that.\"", "tools": "Miro or FigJam; Notion; Claude for alternative framings"}, {"name": "Assumption mapping", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager or UX Researcher", "desc": "List and rank the assumptions underneath the product hypothesis so you know what needs testing first.", "pitfalls": "Only mapping the obvious ones · Trying to test everything at once · Confusing facts with assumptions · Leaving \"the AI can do this\" off the list", "ai": "Generate comprehensive assumption lists · Suggest prioritization · Surface implicit assumptions you missed", "human": "Final prioritization · Acceptable risk · Alignment on what to validate", "check": "The riskiest assumption is written at the top, and everyone on the team names the same one.", "tools": "Miro or FigJam assumption map; Notion"}, {"name": "Research synthesis", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Witness: UX Researcher", "desc": "Combine findings from all the research into insights and recommendations the team will act on.", "pitfalls": "Cherry picking data that supports existing beliefs · Over indexing on one interview · Synthesis documents nobody reads · Letting the AI's pattern become the finding without checking it against the raw notes", "ai": "Find patterns across large amounts of data · Draft the synthesis · Visualize findings", "human": "Interpreting nuance and contradiction · Judgment about what matters · Telling the story so people act", "check": "Every insight traces back to at least two real people, and the team can name them.", "tools": "Dovetail, Notably, or Marvin; Claude for first pass coding of transcripts; Miro for affinity mapping"}, {"name": "Draft the Product North Star", "new": true, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Design Lead, with The Witness", "desc": "Write the first version of the thesis, principles, anti patterns, and the specific user, from what Discovery found.", "pitfalls": "A brand statement with no teeth · Principles so generic any product could claim them · Writing it before understanding the user's pain · Writing it once and never rereading it · Writing it after the design, to justify the design", "ai": "Draft from research synthesis · Stress test principles by proposing decisions they would have to resolve · Generate candidate anti patterns from the competitive landscape", "human": "The thesis · Every principle and every anti pattern · The decision that it is good enough to bind the work", "check": "Two people pick a real design decision from the project and the North Star gives the same answer to both of them. And a real user, shown the thesis, says some version of \"this product sees me.\"", "tools": "A markdown file in the repository so agents read it (NORTH_STAR.md, referenced from CLAUDE.md); Notion for the working draft"}, {"name": "Intelligence mapping", "new": true, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Medium: AI Product Designer", "desc": "Map where in the user's workflow intelligence could carry real weight, where it would corrode trust, and where the answer is not yet known.", "pitfalls": "Starting with \"where can we put a chatbot\" · Using a model for a problem a function could solve, and paying in cost, latency, and unpredictability for it · Assuming capability without testing it · Treating every placement as equally risky · Forgetting where the human must stay in the loop by law or by ethics", "ai": "Propose placements from the workflow map · Estimate feasibility for each · Surface comparable products and their failure reports", "human": "The trust judgment for each placement · Which placements go to Validation for a capability spike · Which are ruled out", "check": "Every candidate placement has a stated trust stake and a next step, and at least one is marked \"not here.\"", "tools": "Miro for the workflow map; Anthropic Console workbench for quick capability probes; Claude"}], "2": [{"name": "Design validation experiments", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager", "desc": "Structure experiments to test the riskiest assumptions before investing in building.", "pitfalls": "Testing what you already know · Experiments that cannot fail · Tests so complex they are inconclusive", "ai": "Suggest experiment designs · Help define metrics and criteria · Generate variations and scenarios", "human": "Which assumptions to test · Risk thresholds · The commitment to act on results", "check": "Before the experiment runs, the result that would kill the idea is written down and agreed.", "tools": "Miro; Notion; Claude for experiment design options"}, {"name": "Build a landing page or minimal test", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager, with any builder", "desc": "A minimal presence, a concierge service, or a faked feature to test demand and gather real signals of intent.", "pitfalls": "Over building until it is no longer minimal · Aesthetics over clarity · Not driving enough traffic to learn anything · Letting the agent add features while building the page", "ai": "Write copy and layouts · Build the page or prototype · Set up analytics · Create variations", "human": "The core message · Traffic and promotion · Interpreting the signals", "check": "You can state the number of real people who took the intent action, and what you decided because of it.", "tools": "Framer or Webflow for a fast page; v0 or Lovable for a coded one; Vercel; Tally or Typeform for intent capture; PostHog or Plausible"}, {"name": "Prototype creation", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: UX Designer, with The Artisan", "desc": "Build interactive prototypes, often coded, that let users experience the core flows before full development.", "pitfalls": "Too polished, so users think it is real · Too rough, so users cannot engage · Prototyping features instead of user problems · Letting the prototype become the product without going back through Design and Architecture", "ai": "Generate UI variations · Write the prototype code · Create realistic sample content · Suggest interaction patterns", "human": "The flow and the experience · Visual language and brand · Fidelity decisions · Steering every turn against the research", "check": "A user can complete the core task without anyone explaining what to do.", "tools": "Claude Code for coded prototypes; v0, Lovable, or Figma Make for fast UI; Figma for design; Vercel previews to share"}, {"name": "Capability spike", "new": true, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Medium: AI Engineer or Evaluation Lead", "desc": "Test whether the model can reliably do the thing the product depends on, using real inputs, real edge cases, and realistic volume.", "pitfalls": "Testing on the three examples that inspired the idea · Judging from a demo · Ignoring latency and cost · Calling a prompt a solution", "ai": "Build the spike harness · Generate adversarial and edge case inputs · Run and tabulate results", "human": "The reliability bar · Which failures are acceptable and which are disqualifying · The go or no go", "check": "You can state the pass rate on a named set of real inputs, and the cases it failed, and someone outside the team would accept that number.", "tools": "Anthropic Console workbench; a small script on the Anthropic SDK; promptfoo or DeepEval for scoring; Langfuse or Braintrust for logging runs"}, {"name": "Run experiments", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager or Growth", "desc": "Execute the validation tests and gather data on real behavior and interest.", "pitfalls": "Stopping too early · Running too long after the answer is clear · Changing the experiment when you see early results", "ai": "Monitor metrics · Flag when you have enough data · Organize the data", "human": "Running it · Qualitative interpretation · When to stop or pivot", "check": "The experiment ran as designed, and the log shows when and why it stopped.", "tools": "PostHog; Tally; Airtable or Notion for the experiment log"}, {"name": "Analyze results", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager", "desc": "Interpret the experiment data to decide what you learned and what it means.", "pitfalls": "Rationalizing results to fit the desired outcome · Ignoring qualitative feedback · Concluding from too little data · Asking the AI to find the good news", "ai": "Statistical analysis and visualization · Pattern finding · Hypotheses from the data", "human": "Interpretation in context · Judgment on ambiguous data · What to do next", "check": "The write up states plainly which assumptions survived and which died, using the criteria set before the experiment.", "tools": "PostHog; Google Sheets; Claude for analysis; Dovetail for the qualitative side"}, {"name": "Pivot or persevere", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "Lab Lead and project lead, with The Questioner", "desc": "Decide whether to continue, change direction significantly, or stop.", "pitfalls": "Continuing out of emotional investment · Pivoting too fast on thin data · Half measures instead of a real pivot", "ai": "Summarize evidence for and against · Model pivot scenarios · Research alternative directions", "human": "The decision; this is leadership · Alignment with vision and values · Telling the team", "check": "The decision is written down with the evidence that drove it, and the North Star was updated or confirmed as a result.", "tools": "A decision record in Notion or the repository; Loom to communicate it"}], "3": [{"name": "Information architecture", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: UX Designer", "desc": "Define how information is organized, labeled, and structured in the product.", "pitfalls": "Navigation that mirrors internal structure · Too many levels · Jargon users do not understand", "ai": "Generate IA options · Analyze how comparable products structure navigation · Suggest conventions", "human": "Understanding mental models from research · Testing the IA (card sorting, tree testing) · Final structure", "check": "Users asked to find three things find them without guidance.", "tools": "Optimal Workshop for card sorting and tree testing; Whimsical or Miro"}, {"name": "User flow design", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: UX Designer", "desc": "Map the paths users take to accomplish key tasks.", "pitfalls": "Happy path only · Too many options · Ignoring the difference between phone and desktop", "ai": "Generate flow variations · Find missing edge cases · Suggest patterns from similar products", "human": "User goals and context · Which flows matter most · Testing with real users", "check": "For the core task, you can state the number of steps and every point where a user could get lost, and each has a way back.", "tools": "FigJam, Whimsical, or Miro; Figma"}, {"name": "Wireframing", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: UX Designer", "desc": "Low fidelity layouts that settle content and function before visual styling. Many teams go straight to a coded prototype here; the goal is the same.", "pitfalls": "Too much visual detail too early · Not exploring alternatives · Skipping straight to visuals", "ai": "Generate layout variations quickly · Suggest patterns · Create placeholder content", "human": "Content strategy and priority · Validating with users · Resolving feedback", "check": "At least two structurally different layouts were considered, and you can say why the chosen one won.", "tools": "Figma; Balsamiq; v0 or Claude Code for coded wireframes"}, {"name": "Visual design", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Artisan: Visual Designer", "desc": "Color, typography, imagery, and styling that create the product's look and feel.", "pitfalls": "Aesthetics over usability · Inconsistent visual language across screens · Ignoring accessibility · Accepting the AI's default palette and type as a choice", "ai": "Palette and type variations · Placeholder imagery · Consistency checks across screens", "human": "Brand expression and emotional tone · Taste · Ensuring the design serves the user", "check": "Screenshot a screen with the logo hidden. Someone who knows the product can tell it is this product.", "tools": "Figma; Claude Code with a frontend design skill; Google Fonts or Fontshare; Realtime Colors"}, {"name": "Interaction design", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Artisan: Motion Designer, with The Architect", "desc": "How users interact with elements and how the system responds.", "pitfalls": "Animation that slows people down · Inconsistent patterns · No feedback while loading or processing", "ai": "Suggest patterns · Document micro interactions · Draft motion specifications", "human": "Feel and timing · Whether the interaction helps · Testing with real users", "check": "Every user action has visible feedback within a beat, and turning motion off breaks nothing.", "tools": "Figma prototyping; Framer; Motion (framer-motion) for coded motion; LottieFiles"}, {"name": "Content design", "new": true, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Artisan: Content Designer, with The Witness", "desc": "Define the product's voice and write the words in the interface: labels, empty states, errors, confirmations, onboarding.", "pitfalls": "Copy written last, by whoever is closest · Generic system tone · Different voices on different screens · Model output with no voice at all", "ai": "Draft copy at scale from the voice definition · Flag inconsistency across screens · Propose alternatives", "human": "The voice definition · Every word a user will read at a moment of stress · Final review of every surface", "check": "Read the error messages aloud. They sound like the same product as the onboarding.", "tools": "A voice document in Notion; Claude for drafts; Hemingway for plain language; Figma for in context copy"}, {"name": "Designing the AI experience", "new": true, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Medium: AI Product Designer, with The Witness", "desc": "Design how the intelligence shows up: what it does on its own, what it asks first, how it shows confidence, how the user corrects it, and what happens when it is wrong.", "pitfalls": "A chat box as the only interface · Swaying too far in either direction on the autonomy gradient: an AI that asks permission for everything and becomes a nag, or one that acts on things it should have asked about · Hiding uncertainty to look polished · No way to undo an AI action · Trust designed as a feature instead of earned as a behavior", "ai": "Generate interaction variations · Simulate failure and low confidence states · Draft explanation copy", "human": "The autonomy gradient · What the user can always override · Whether the product is honest about what it does not know", "check": "For every AI action, you can point to the screen where the user sees it was wrong and fixes it in one step.", "tools": "Figma; coded prototypes in Claude Code; Vercel AI SDK UI components; shadcn/ui"}, {"name": "Design system creation", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Artisan: Design Lead, with The Architect", "desc": "A library of reusable components, patterns, and guidelines, in design and in code.", "pitfalls": "Over engineering before you know what you need · No usage guidance · Components too rigid · Agents inventing one off components instead of using the system", "ai": "Generate component variations · Write documentation · Find patterns in existing designs · Build the coded components", "human": "The core decisions and principles · Governance and contribution · Adoption", "check": "An agent given a new screen to build uses only system components, and the result is indistinguishable from a human built screen.", "tools": "Figma variables and libraries; shadcn/ui on Tailwind; Storybook; Style Dictionary for tokens; Penpot as the open source option"}, {"name": "Prototype testing", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Witness: UX Researcher", "desc": "Test designs with real users to see whether the solution meets their needs.", "pitfalls": "Only internal testers · Leading users to the right answer · Testing too late to change anything", "ai": "Draft scripts and tasks · Transcribe and analyze sessions · Compile findings", "human": "Running the sessions · Rapport · Real time interpretation and follow up", "check": "Something the team was sure about turned out to be confusing, and the design changed.", "tools": "Maze for unmoderated; Zoom or Lookback for moderated; UserTesting for panel recruits; Dovetail"}, {"name": "Accessibility review", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Witness: Accessibility Specialist", "desc": "Check that designs meet accessibility standards and work for people with disabilities before they are built.", "pitfalls": "Accessibility as an afterthought · Trusting automated checks alone · Forgetting cognitive accessibility", "ai": "Scan designs for common issues · Suggest alt text · Check contrast automatically", "human": "Understanding diverse needs · Manual testing with assistive technology · Balancing accessibility with design intent", "check": "Every screen has a documented keyboard path and reading order, and the contrast check passed on the real palette.", "tools": "Stark for Figma; WebAIM contrast checker; Figma contrast plugin"}], "4": [{"name": "Technology selection", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Software Architect, with The Groundskeeper", "desc": "Choose the languages, frameworks, and tools that form the technical foundation. Start from the Lab's defaults and deviate on purpose.", "pitfalls": "Choosing what is trendy · Ignoring what the team actually knows · Over engineering for hypothetical scale · Letting the agent pick the stack it likes", "ai": "Research options and tradeoffs · Compare frameworks · Analyze ecosystem activity", "human": "Fit with team skills and growth · Long term direction · Buy in on the choices", "check": "For every deviation from the Lab defaults, there is a written reason, and the maintainer succession plan can live with it.", "tools": "Lab defaults (Next.js on Vercel, Supabase, Anthropic API, GitHub); npm trends; an architecture decision record in the repository"}, {"name": "System design", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Software Architect", "desc": "The high level structure: components, boundaries, and how they interact.", "pitfalls": "Optimizing for scale you do not have · Distributed systems before you need them · Forgetting operational needs", "ai": "Generate diagrams · Suggest patterns for common problems · Review the design for issues", "human": "Tradeoffs based on what the product needs · Non functional requirements · Long term maintainability", "check": "The design fits on one page, and every arrow on it has an owner and a failure mode.", "tools": "Excalidraw or Miro; Mermaid diagrams committed to the repository; C4 model; Claude Code"}, {"name": "Database design", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: Software Architect", "desc": "The data model, the database choice, and the access patterns.", "pitfalls": "Databases chosen on hype · Query patterns ignored until they are slow · Migration needs ignored · Storing data the privacy design said not to store", "ai": "Generate schema designs from requirements · Suggest indexing · Review for common issues", "human": "The requirements · Consistency and integrity · Privacy decisions", "check": "Every table can be explained by a user facing need, and the privacy design signs off on every field.", "tools": "Supabase (Postgres); dbdiagram.io or DrawSQL; Drizzle or Prisma; Supabase migrations"}, {"name": "API design", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: Software Architect", "desc": "The interfaces between systems, internal and external.", "pitfalls": "Exposing internals · Inconsistent naming · No plan for evolution", "ai": "Generate specifications · Suggest conventions · Write documentation", "human": "What consumers need · What to expose · Versioning strategy", "check": "A developer who has never seen the system can call the core endpoint correctly from the docs alone.", "tools": "OpenAPI with Swagger Editor; Next.js route handlers or tRPC; Zod for schemas"}, {"name": "Security architecture", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Security, with The Long Game", "desc": "Authentication, authorization, and data protection, designed in.", "pitfalls": "Security as an afterthought · Rolling your own crypto or auth · Ignoring the human attack surface · Guardrails that live only in a prompt", "ai": "Research patterns · Review the design for vulnerabilities · Draft security documentation", "human": "Threat modeling · Architecture decisions · Regulatory requirements", "check": "There is a written threat model, and for each threat, a control and an owner.", "tools": "OWASP ASVS as the checklist; OWASP Threat Dragon or Miro for the threat model; Supabase row level security; Supabase Auth or Clerk"}, {"name": "AI architecture", "new": true, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Medium: AI Engineer or Prompt and Harness Engineer", "desc": "Decide the model, the harness (tools, guardrails, retrieval, memory), the evaluation approach, the cost budget, and the fallback when the model is unavailable or wrong.", "pitfalls": "The newest model by default · No guardrails · No evals · No fallback · Cost discovered from the invoice", "ai": "Compare models against spike results · Draft the harness · Propose an eval plan · Model cost at projected volume", "human": "The never list · The cost ceiling · The fallback behavior · The choice of model", "check": "You can answer, in one sentence each: what the model does, why this model was chosen over the alternatives, what it is never allowed to do, what it costs at ten times current usage, and what the user sees when it is down.", "tools": "Anthropic API; Vercel AI SDK; Anthropic Console for prompts; Langfuse or Braintrust for tracing; a cost model in a spreadsheet; Helicone or Portkey as a gateway if needed"}, {"name": "Privacy and data design", "new": true, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Long Game: Privacy and Compliance, with The Architect: Security", "desc": "Decide what user data is collected, why, where it lives, who can see it, how long it is kept, and how a user deletes it completely.", "pitfalls": "Deferring privacy to Scale · Storing a copy of something the user already has, because it was convenient · Sending user data to a model without a decision to do so · Retention by accident · No way to delete a user", "ai": "Draft the data inventory · Map jurisdictions that apply · Draft retention and deletion logic", "human": "What is collected and why · What is sent to models · The deletion guarantee · Sign off with whoever holds this responsibility for the organization", "check": "A user asks to be deleted and you can show, from the code, that they are gone from every store including third parties. And for every stored field, someone can say why the product could not work without it.", "tools": "Supabase region and row level security settings; a data inventory in Notion; Termly or iubenda for policies; the framework's privacy checklist"}, {"name": "Infrastructure planning", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Midnight Responder: DevOps or Platform Engineer", "desc": "Hosting, networking, environments, and the infrastructure that runs it all.", "pitfalls": "Over provisioning · No disaster recovery plan · Manual setup nobody can reproduce", "ai": "Generate infrastructure as code · Compare services · Estimate costs", "human": "Budget · Vendor selection · Who is operationally responsible", "check": "The whole environment can be rebuilt from the repository, and someone has done it.", "tools": "Vercel; Supabase; Cloudflare; Doppler or Vercel environment variables for secrets; Terraform or Pulumi only if the project needs it"}, {"name": "CI/CD setup", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Groundskeeper: DevOps Engineer", "desc": "Automated pipelines for building, testing, and deploying.", "pitfalls": "\"Automation later\" · Pipelines so slow people skip them · No security scanning · Gates that can be bypassed", "ai": "Generate the configuration · Set up testing and linting · Configure deployment", "human": "Deployment strategy and approvals · Secrets and environments · Fit with how the team works", "check": "A deliberately broken test blocks a deploy, and a rollback has been executed at least once on purpose.", "tools": "GitHub Actions; Vercel preview deployments; Dependabot; Semgrep; Playwright in CI"}, {"name": "Development environment setup", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Groundskeeper: Software Engineer", "desc": "Consistent, reproducible development environments for everyone on the team, including the agents.", "pitfalls": "\"Works on my machine\" · Complex manual setup · Drift from production · Agent instructions that differ between team members", "ai": "Generate container configuration · Write setup scripts and docs · Troubleshoot issues", "human": "What to standardize versus leave flexible · Supporting the team · Keeping docs current", "check": "A new team member is productive on day one from the docs alone.", "tools": "Dev containers or Docker; pnpm; ESLint and Prettier; CLAUDE.md for agent instructions; Doppler for shared env"}, {"name": "Bootstrap the Foundation", "new": true, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Groundskeeper: Software Engineer", "desc": "Set up the living documentation system from the Lab template: the index, the first sub docs, the cross system map, and the pre push hook.", "pitfalls": "Documenting the plan instead of the code · Skipping the hook · Sub docs so long they get skipped · Treating the docs as a deliverable instead of a working surface", "ai": "Generate the structure from the template · Draft the first sub docs from the architecture plan · Write and install the hook", "human": "Which systems get a sub doc · Reviewing the first sub docs for accuracy · The decision that the hook is binding", "check": "A new agent, given only the index and a task, loads the right sub docs and states the ripple correctly.", "tools": "The Lab template (markdown in the repository); Husky or lefthook for the pre push hook; Claude Code"}, {"name": "Agent readable build spec", "new": true, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Software Architect, with The Questioner: Product Manager", "desc": "Translate the design and the North Star into a specification an agent can execute: scope, constraints, interfaces, what done means, what is out of bounds.", "pitfalls": "A vague prompt in place of a spec · Scope that grows because the agent noticed something · No definition of done · Writing the spec after the code", "ai": "Draft the spec from the design artifacts · Find gaps and ambiguities · Propose the increment breakdown", "human": "Scope and constraints · Every acceptance check · The judgment that the spec is complete enough to hand off", "check": "Two different agents given the spec build recognizably the same thing.", "tools": "SPEC.md in the repository; Claude Code plan mode; Linear or GitHub Issues for increments"}], "5": [{"name": "Implement against the spec", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Architect: Software Engineer, with The Groundskeeper", "desc": "Turn the build spec into working code across every layer: interface, server logic, APIs, and data. In an agentic workflow the split by layer matters less than the split by verification, so this is one activity with four acceptance checks.", "pitfalls": "Coding before understanding · Scope that grows because the agent noticed something · Accepting output you cannot explain · Deviating from the design without a conversation · Deviating from the API spec without updating it · Migrations that cannot be reversed · Security holes in generated server code · \"It works\" on the demo path only", "ai": "The implementation, end to end, in increments · Boilerplate and scaffolding · Debugging", "human": "The spec · Review of every increment · The four acceptance checks · Migration safety · Security review of server code · Code quality judgment", "check": "Four checks. Interface: every screen matches the design, uses only system components, and has loading, empty, and error states. Server: every error path is handled and logged, and you can explain what the code does. API: the implementation matches the spec, and the spec was updated if anything changed. Data: every migration has been rolled back in staging and the indexes match the real access patterns.", "tools": "Claude Code, with Cursor or GitHub Copilot for inline work; Next.js; Supabase; GitHub pull requests; Vercel previews"}, {"name": "Prompt and harness development", "new": true, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Medium: Prompt and Harness Engineer", "desc": "Build the prompts, tool definitions, guardrails, and retrieval that turn the model into the feature.", "pitfalls": "Prompt tweaks with no eval · Guardrails in the prompt only · Silent prompt drift across surfaces · No logging", "ai": "Draft and refine prompts · Generate tool schemas · Propose guardrail logic", "human": "Review of every prompt and harness change · The never list · What gets logged", "check": "Every prompt change has a diff, a reviewer, and an eval run attached.", "tools": "Anthropic API; Vercel AI SDK; prompts versioned in the repository; Langfuse prompt management; Zod for tool schemas"}, {"name": "Writing tests", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Groundskeeper: QA Engineer or Software Engineer", "desc": "Automated tests that prove behavior and catch regressions.", "pitfalls": "Tests coupled to implementation · Edge cases untested · Flaky or slow tests · Tests the human cannot say the purpose of · Tests that pass because the expected values were pasted from the output, or that mock so much they prove nothing", "ai": "Generate cases and scenarios · Write unit and integration tests · Find untested paths", "human": "Test strategy and what gets covered · The critical business logic tests · Test quality over time", "check": "You can say what each test proves, the tests test behavior rather than implementation, nothing in them is hard coded to pass, and a deliberately introduced bug on a critical path fails the suite.", "tools": "Vitest; Playwright; React Testing Library; Claude Code"}, {"name": "Eval writing", "new": true, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Medium: Evaluation Lead, with The Groundskeeper", "desc": "The evaluation suite for AI behavior: the cases, the expected outcomes, the scoring, and the thresholds that block a release.", "pitfalls": "Evals that only cover the happy path · Evals the model helped write and the human never examined · Thresholds nobody enforces · Running once", "ai": "Generate cases from logs and spike results · Write the harness · Run and report", "human": "The cases that matter most · The thresholds · Reading the failures", "check": "The eval suite fails a release you deliberately break, and the team can name what each failing case proves.", "tools": "promptfoo; DeepEval; Langfuse or Braintrust datasets; GitHub Actions to run in CI"}, {"name": "Code review", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Software Engineer, reviewing each other", "desc": "Review changes for correctness, quality, and shared understanding. With agents writing most of the code, review is where human understanding of the codebase is built.", "pitfalls": "Rubber stamping · Style nitpicks only · Harsh or personal feedback · Approving agent output because it is long and looks thorough", "ai": "Initial analysis · Check for common issues · Suggest improvements · Summarize the change", "human": "Context and intent · Design and architecture feedback · Mentoring", "check": "The reviewer can explain the change to someone else without looking at it.", "tools": "GitHub pull requests; CodeRabbit or Claude Code review as a first pass"}, {"name": "Accessibility implementation", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Witness: Accessibility Specialist, with The Architect: Software Engineer", "desc": "Keyboard navigation, screen reader support, focus management, and semantics, built in.", "pitfalls": "ARIA everywhere instead of semantics · Broken keyboard navigation · Never testing with a screen reader · Trusting the agent's claim that a component is accessible", "ai": "Generate accessible component code · Suggest patterns · Find issues automatically", "human": "Testing with assistive technology · Understanding diverse needs · The actual experience", "check": "Someone completed the core task with the screen off, using a screen reader and the keyboard only.", "tools": "Radix primitives via shadcn/ui; axe DevTools; eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y; VoiceOver and NVDA"}, {"name": "Analytics instrumentation", "new": true, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager or Data Analyst", "desc": "Instrument the product so that Launch and Learn have data to look at: the core actions, the funnel, the errors, and the AI outcomes.", "pitfalls": "Instrumenting everything and learning nothing · Deciding what to measure after launch · Events that leak user data · No AI outcome events", "ai": "Implement the event plan · Validate events fire · Build the first dashboards", "human": "The questions · The event plan · Review against the privacy design", "check": "Before launch, you can state the one number you will look at on day one and show the dashboard that displays it.", "tools": "PostHog; Vercel Analytics; a tracking plan in Notion"}, {"name": "Technical documentation", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Witness: Technical Writer, with The Groundskeeper", "desc": "Documentation for code, APIs, and systems, kept alongside the Foundation.", "pitfalls": "Docs that rot · Too much or too little · Docs nobody can find · Documentation generated from code that nobody checked", "ai": "Generate from code · Write READMEs and API docs · Create examples", "human": "What needs documenting · Accuracy review · Keeping it current", "check": "Pick any doc at random. The code it describes still does that.", "tools": "Markdown in the repository; README; Mintlify or Docusaurus if public; Claude Code"}, {"name": "Foundation upkeep", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Groundskeeper: Software Engineer", "desc": "Keep the living documentation current with every change, as the pre push hook requires. This is the \"after\" step of the Foundation workflow, done continuously through Build.", "pitfalls": "Skipping the update because the change was small · Bypassing the hook · Flags left dangling", "ai": "Draft the doc updates from the diff · Detect which sub docs the change touches", "human": "Confirming the ripple was named correctly · Deciding a flag is truly resolved", "check": "The hook has never been bypassed, and the index's last reviewed dates are all within the current sprint.", "tools": "Husky or lefthook hook; Claude Code; markdown in the repository"}], "6": [{"name": "Load testing", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Midnight Responder: Platform Engineer, with The Architect: Performance", "desc": "Test performance under expected and peak load.", "pitfalls": "Unrealistic scenarios · Testing the API but not the full system · No baseline · Forgetting that AI calls have rate limits and bills", "ai": "Write the scripts · Analyze results · Identify bottlenecks", "human": "Defining realistic load · Interpreting in context · Which optimizations to prioritize", "check": "You can state the load at which the product degrades, what degrades first, and what it would cost per month at that load.", "tools": "k6 or Artillery; Vercel and Supabase dashboards; Anthropic Console usage"}, {"name": "Security testing", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Security", "desc": "Test for injection, authentication, authorization, and data exposure flaws.", "pitfalls": "Automated scanners only · Authorization edge cases untested · Third party dependency risk ignored", "ai": "Run scans · Identify vulnerability patterns · Generate test cases", "human": "The specific threat model · Business logic vulnerabilities · Prioritizing and fixing", "check": "Every finding has a fix or a written, signed acceptance of the risk.", "tools": "OWASP ZAP; Semgrep; Snyk or npm audit; Supabase security advisors"}, {"name": "AI red teaming", "new": true, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Medium: AI Engineer, with The Architect: Security", "desc": "Attack the AI surfaces on purpose: prompt injection, data exfiltration through the model, jailbreaks of the never list, and unsafe outputs.", "pitfalls": "Assuming the prompt protects you · Testing only with polite inputs · Forgetting tool access is attack surface · No output validation", "ai": "Generate attack inputs · Run them at volume · Classify results", "human": "Which failures block launch · Fixes to the harness · Sign off", "check": "You have a written list of attacks that were tried, which ones succeeded, and the fix for each, and the never list holds under all of them.", "tools": "promptfoo red teaming; DeepTeam; Garak; Lakera Guard or Prompt Guard for injection detection; a written attack list"}, {"name": "Penetration testing", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Architect: Security specialist, often external", "desc": "Have a security expert attempt to breach the system.", "pitfalls": "Checkbox exercise · Poor scoping · Findings never fixed", "ai": "Prepare for the test · Help remediate · Document findings and fixes", "human": "The testing itself · Creative attack thinking · Prioritizing remediation", "check": "The tester's report exists, and every critical finding is closed.", "tools": "An external tester; Burp Suite; Cobalt or HackerOne if budget allows"}, {"name": "Accessibility audit", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Witness: Accessibility Specialist", "desc": "A full review of accessibility compliance and real usability.", "pitfalls": "Automated tools only · No real users · Treating it as a one time event", "ai": "Run scans · Generate compliance reports · Suggest fixes", "human": "Manual testing with assistive tech · Understanding the experience · Prioritizing fixes", "check": "At least one person who uses assistive technology daily completed the core task and told you what was hard.", "tools": "axe; Lighthouse; WAVE; Accessibility Insights; VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS"}, {"name": "Error handling review", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Midnight Responder: Error Handling, with The Artisan: Content Designer", "desc": "Review how the system handles errors, failures, and edge cases, and what the user sees when it does.", "pitfalls": "Generic messages · Stack traces shown to users · Silent failures · Model failures that look like success", "ai": "Find gaps · Suggest patterns · Review messages for clarity", "human": "What users need to know versus what to hide · Actionable errors · Degradation strategy", "check": "For every failure the load test and the red team found, you can show the screen the user would see.", "tools": "Sentry; log review in Vercel and Supabase; Claude Code"}, {"name": "Privacy review", "new": true, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Long Game: Privacy and Compliance", "desc": "Verify the privacy and data design was built as designed, and that nothing slipped in during Build.", "pitfalls": "Reviewing the design instead of the build · Trusting the diagram · Forgetting the logs", "ai": "Trace data flows in the code · Diff against the design · Run deletion tests", "human": "Sign off · Any decision to accept a gap", "check": "The person responsible for user privacy at the organization would sign it.", "tools": "A data flow diagram in Miro; Supabase and PostHog data settings; log audit; the privacy checklist"}, {"name": "Monitoring setup", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Monitoring, with The Midnight Responder", "desc": "Observability for system health, performance, behavior, and AI outcomes.", "pitfalls": "Monitoring everything and seeing nothing · Infrastructure only · Not set up before launch · No AI metrics", "ai": "Generate configuration · Suggest metrics · Build dashboards", "human": "What matters · Objectives and thresholds · Reviewing and acting on the data", "check": "Someone can look at one dashboard and say whether the product is healthy right now.", "tools": "Sentry; Vercel observability; Supabase logs; Better Stack or Grafana Cloud; Langfuse for model metrics"}, {"name": "Alerting configuration", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Midnight Responder: On call Engineer", "desc": "Alerts that reach the right person when something needs attention.", "pitfalls": "Alert fatigue · Alerts nobody acts on · Missing the critical ones", "ai": "Suggest rules · Generate configuration · Analyze alert patterns", "human": "What needs immediate attention · On call and response · Tuning", "check": "Every alert has a named human who will receive it and a runbook they can follow.", "tools": "Better Stack or PagerDuty; Sentry alerts; Slack"}, {"name": "Runbook creation", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Midnight Responder: whoever built the system and whoever will be paged", "desc": "Procedures for responding to likely operational issues, drafted from the Foundation.", "pitfalls": "Assumes too much · Procedures that do not work · Out of date", "ai": "Draft from the Foundation and the alert list · Write troubleshooting steps · Keep runbooks consistent", "human": "System knowledge · Testing that the procedures work · Keeping them current", "check": "Someone who did not write the runbook followed it under a simulated incident and recovered the system.", "tools": "Markdown in the repository, drafted by Claude Code from the Foundation; Notion"}, {"name": "Disaster recovery testing", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Midnight Responder: Platform Engineer", "desc": "Test backup, restore, and failover to prove they work.", "pitfalls": "Backups never restored · Undocumented recovery · Unrealistic test conditions", "ai": "Generate test plans · Document procedures · Automate backup verification", "human": "Running the tests · Decisions during incidents · Improving from results", "check": "You have restored production from backup, on purpose, and you can state how long it took and what was lost.", "tools": "Supabase backups and point in time recovery; Vercel instant rollback; the runbook"}, {"name": "User beta", "new": true, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Witness: UX Researcher, with Member Success", "desc": "Put the product in the hands of a small group of real users before launch, watch them use it, and fix what they trip on.", "pitfalls": "Beta with friends of the team only · Explaining the product to beta users · Treating feedback as votes · Launching on schedule regardless", "ai": "Nothing in the core act. Synthesis of notes afterward.", "human": "Everything", "check": "At least one thing the team was sure about turned out to be wrong, and it was fixed before launch.", "tools": "Vercel preview or a beta flag in PostHog; a Slack channel; Tally for feedback; Dovetail for notes"}], "7": [{"name": "Launch planning", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager, coordinating everyone", "desc": "The plan for launch: timing, people, criteria, contingencies.", "pitfalls": "Support not prepared · No success criteria · No rollback procedure", "ai": "Generate checklists · Timeline templates · Draft communications", "human": "Coordination · The go or no go · Stakeholder communication", "check": "The go or no go criteria are written, and every person named in the plan knows what they are doing on launch day.", "tools": "Notion or Linear; the release checklist"}, {"name": "Staged rollout", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Groundskeeper: Release Manager", "desc": "Release to increasing numbers of users while watching for issues.", "pitfalls": "Expanding too fast · Not watching · No rollback automation", "ai": "Configure feature flags · Monitor rollout metrics · Automate expansion", "human": "Expand or pause · Interpreting anomalies · Communicating status", "check": "Each expansion step was a decision someone made from data, and the log shows it.", "tools": "PostHog feature flags; Vercel Edge Config; LaunchDarkly for larger products"}, {"name": "Monitoring during launch", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Midnight Responder: On call Engineer", "desc": "Actively watch the system during launch to catch and respond fast.", "pitfalls": "Nobody actually watching · Alert fatigue at the worst time · Slow response", "ai": "Anomaly detection · Correlate events across systems · Surface relevant logs", "human": "What is concerning · Act or wait · Coordination", "check": "A named person watched the dashboards for the full launch window, and the log of what they saw exists.", "tools": "Sentry; PostHog live events; Vercel; a Better Stack status page"}, {"name": "User support triage", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Witness: Community Support", "desc": "Handle incoming questions, issues, and feedback during and after launch.", "pitfalls": "Support unprepared for new features · Patterns not tracked · Slow responses", "ai": "Draft templates · Categorize and route · Find patterns", "human": "Direct communication · Empathy · Escalation decisions", "check": "Every user who reported a problem in the first week heard back from a human.", "tools": "Slack; Plain, Intercom, or Help Scout; a Notion FAQ"}, {"name": "Quick fixes and hotfixes", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: Software Engineer, with The Groundskeeper", "desc": "Rapidly fix critical issues found during launch.", "pitfalls": "Skipping tests in the rush · New bugs from hasty fixes · Undocumented changes · Letting the agent \"fix\" more than the bug", "ai": "Find the root cause fast · Generate the fix · Test it", "human": "What needs immediate fixing · Review under pressure · Communication about outages", "check": "Every hotfix has a test that would have caught the bug, and a note in the Foundation.", "tools": "GitHub; Vercel instant rollback; Claude Code"}, {"name": "Communication", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager, with Community Support", "desc": "Keep users and stakeholders informed about status, issues, and wins.", "pitfalls": "Under communicating during problems · Inconsistent messaging · Not acknowledging what went wrong", "ai": "Draft status updates · Prepare FAQs · Draft announcements", "human": "Tone and sensitivity · What to share and when · Direct conversations with key people", "check": "A user who hit a problem on launch day can find out what happened and what was done about it without asking.", "tools": "Slack; Resend or Loops for email; WAAI's community platform; a Better Stack status page"}, {"name": "Launch retrospective", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The whole team, facilitated by the Lab Lead or project lead", "desc": "Review the launch process to learn and improve for next time.", "pitfalls": "Skipping it because \"it went fine\" · Blaming individuals · Not acting on what was learned", "ai": "Compile metrics and timeline · Organize feedback · Track action items", "human": "Facilitating honesty · Psychological safety · Committing to change", "check": "At least one thing about the Lab's own process changed as a result, and it is written into the program docs.", "tools": "A FigJam or Miro retro board; Notion"}], "8": [{"name": "Analytics review", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager or Data Analyst", "desc": "Analyze usage data to understand behavior and performance.", "pitfalls": "Drowning in data · Vanity metrics · Data disconnected from decisions · Accepting the AI's narrative of the data without looking at the chart", "ai": "Reports and visualizations · Patterns and anomalies · Correlate sources", "human": "The questions · Interpretation in context · Insights into actions", "check": "The review names one thing the team believed at launch that the data contradicts.", "tools": "PostHog; Vercel Analytics; Google Sheets; Claude"}, {"name": "User interview rounds", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Witness: UX Researcher", "desc": "Keep talking to users about their experience with the live product.", "pitfalls": "Only happy users · Stopping research after launch · Not acting on it", "ai": "Transcribe and analyze · Patterns across users · Draft guides", "human": "The conversations · Reading between the lines · Ongoing relationships", "check": "You talked to someone who stopped using the product, and you know why.", "tools": "Zoom; Otter or Grain; Calendly; Dovetail"}, {"name": "Support ticket analysis", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Witness: Community Support, with The Questioner", "desc": "Find the patterns in support requests that point to product problems.", "pitfalls": "Counting instead of understanding · Support separate from product · Not closing the loop", "ai": "Categorize and cluster · Spot emerging issues · Trend reports", "human": "Understanding the frustration · What to fix · Connecting support to the roadmap", "check": "The top three ticket categories each have a product change assigned or a written decision not to.", "tools": "Plain or Intercom exports; Claude for clustering; Dovetail"}, {"name": "AI quality review", "new": true, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Medium: Evaluation Lead, with The Witness", "desc": "Read what the model actually did in production: acceptance, correction, abandonment, reported failures, and cost.", "pitfalls": "Aggregate scores only · Never adding production cases to the evals · Cost reviewed by finance, not product", "ai": "Cluster failures · Pull representative examples · Draft new eval cases", "human": "What counts as a failure · Which failures change the product · Whether the intelligence is still in the right seat", "check": "The eval suite is larger than it was at launch, and every addition came from a real user.", "tools": "Langfuse or Braintrust traces; promptfoo or DeepEval regression runs; Anthropic Console cost dashboard"}, {"name": "Funnel analysis", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager or Data Analyst", "desc": "How users move through key flows and where they drop off.", "pitfalls": "Overall funnel only · Not investigating why · Micro conversions that do not matter", "ai": "Build visualizations · Segment and compare · Find significant drop offs", "human": "What matters in the funnel · Why people drop · Priorities", "check": "For the biggest drop off, you have talked to at least one person who dropped there.", "tools": "PostHog funnels"}, {"name": "Cohort analysis", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Questioner: Data Analyst", "desc": "Compare groups of users over time to understand retention and engagement.", "pitfalls": "Cohorts too small to mean anything · Not tracked long enough · Analysis without action", "ai": "Build cohort tables and charts · Find patterns · Compare statistically", "human": "Defining cohorts · Interpreting · Designing interventions", "check": "You can name one thing the retained users did that the churned users did not, and you have a plan to test it.", "tools": "PostHog retention and cohorts; Amplitude for larger products"}, {"name": "Incident retrospectives", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Midnight Responder: the engineering team", "desc": "Review incidents and outages to understand what happened and prevent recurrence.", "pitfalls": "Blame culture · Stopping at the first cause · Not following through", "ai": "Compile the timeline · Document technical detail · Track actions", "human": "Psychological safety · Deep analysis · Cultural improvement", "check": "The write up names at least two contributing factors, and the Foundation was updated with what was learned.", "tools": "Sentry timeline; a Notion postmortem template"}, {"name": "Synthesis and prioritization", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager", "desc": "Combine everything learned into product direction.", "pitfalls": "Chasing every piece of feedback · Ignoring contradicting data · Loudest voices over biggest impact", "ai": "Compile insights · Suggest prioritization frameworks · Model impact", "human": "Strategic judgment · Alignment · Communicating the why", "check": "The team can explain why the top priority is the top priority using evidence from this stage, not opinion.", "tools": "Notion; Linear roadmap"}], "9": [{"name": "Prioritization", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Product Manager, with the project lead", "desc": "Decide what to work on next based on impact, effort, and the North Star.", "pitfalls": "Whoever asks loudest · Never revisiting · Doing everything at once", "ai": "Apply frameworks · Model impact · Compare options", "human": "What matters · Negotiation · Communicating and defending", "check": "The backlog is ordered, the top item traces to a finding from Learn, and the team knows what was deliberately not chosen.", "tools": "Linear or GitHub Projects; a RICE sheet; Notion"}, {"name": "Design iteration", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: UX Designer, with The Artisan", "desc": "Improve designs based on feedback and data from the live product.", "pitfalls": "Redesigning on opinion · Changing too fast to learn · Breaking existing expectations", "ai": "Generate variations · Analyze session recordings · Suggest improvements from patterns", "human": "Judgment and taste · User context · Coherence", "check": "Each design change names the finding that motivated it, and the North Star still holds.", "tools": "Figma; PostHog session replay; Maze"}, {"name": "Feature development", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Architect: Software Engineer, with The Groundskeeper", "desc": "Build new features and improvements from the prioritized backlog. Same discipline as Build: spec first, increments, acceptance checks.", "pitfalls": "No success criteria · Over scoping · Not instrumenting · Skipping the spec because \"it is just a small feature\"", "ai": "The implementation · Technical approaches · Tests", "human": "Architecture and design decisions · Code quality · Speed versus quality", "check": "The feature has a spec, a test, an analytics event, and a Foundation entry, and you can say what number it is meant to move.", "tools": "Claude Code; GitHub; Vercel previews; PostHog events"}, {"name": "Technical debt paydown", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Groundskeeper: Maintenance", "desc": "Address accumulated debt to keep velocity.", "pitfalls": "Ignoring until critical · Gold plating · Not communicating the cost of debt", "ai": "Find code smells · Generate refactoring · Write tests for legacy code", "human": "What debt to address · Acceptable versus problematic · Advocating for the time", "check": "The debt list exists, each item has a cost, and the ones paid down this cycle were the ones blocking the next feature.", "tools": "SonarCloud; GitHub issues; Claude Code"}, {"name": "Refactoring", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Groundskeeper: Software Engineer", "desc": "Improve code structure without changing behavior.", "pitfalls": "Refactoring without tests · Behavior changes sneaking in · Big bang refactors · Letting the agent refactor beyond the scope it was given", "ai": "Suggest patterns · Execute mechanical refactors · Confirm tests pass", "human": "What to refactor · Reviewing the changes · Test adequacy", "check": "The test suite passed before and after with no test changes, and the diff touches only what was scoped.", "tools": "Claude Code; Vitest; Playwright"}, {"name": "A/B testing setup", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Questioner: Growth, with a Software Engineer", "desc": "Infrastructure and experiments to test changes with real users.", "pitfalls": "No hypothesis · Stopping early · Novelty effects", "ai": "Build the infrastructure · Calculate significance · Generate configurations", "human": "The hypothesis · Interpreting results · Ship or iterate", "check": "The hypothesis and the stopping rule were written before the test started.", "tools": "PostHog experiments; Vercel Edge Config flags; Statsig for larger products"}, {"name": "Documentation updates", "new": false, "mode": "ai-leads", "role": "The Witness: Technical Writer, with The Groundskeeper", "desc": "Keep user and technical documentation current with changes.", "pitfalls": "Drift from reality · Forgetting to update · Too much to maintain", "ai": "Generate updates from code changes · Write user documentation · Find outdated docs", "human": "Accuracy · What needs documenting · Telling users about changes", "check": "The docs for the last three features shipped are accurate, and someone other than the author checked.", "tools": "Markdown in the repository; Mintlify or Docusaurus; Claude Code"}], "10": [{"name": "Compliance", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Long Game: Privacy and Compliance", "desc": "Achieve and maintain the security and compliance posture that the organization's partners and jurisdictions require.", "pitfalls": "One time project · Not knowing what auditors need · Last minute scrambles", "ai": "Draft policies and procedures · Track evidence · Generate audit documentation", "human": "Understanding requirements · Implementing real controls · Auditor relationships", "check": "You could answer a partner's security questionnaire today from existing evidence.", "tools": "Vanta or Drata for SOC 2 automation; evidence in Notion"}, {"name": "Internationalization", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Long Game: Ambassador, with a Software Engineer", "desc": "Prepare the product for multiple languages and regions.", "pitfalls": "Hardcoded strings · Plural forms wrong · Non text content forgotten · Model output in one language only", "ai": "Find hardcoded strings · Generate the infrastructure · Extract strings", "human": "Architecture decisions · Cultural considerations · Testing in real locales", "check": "Switching the locale changes every string, every date, and every model response, with nothing left in the source language.", "tools": "next-intl or i18next; ICU MessageFormat; Anthropic API for draft translations"}, {"name": "Localization", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Long Game: Ambassador, with translators and local reviewers from the community", "desc": "Translate and adapt for specific markets.", "pitfalls": "Machine translation without review · Ignoring cultural differences · No budget for ongoing work", "ai": "Draft translations · Find untranslated content · Manage the workflow", "human": "Quality review · Cultural appropriateness · Local understanding", "check": "A native speaker in the target region used the product and did not notice it was translated.", "tools": "Crowdin or Lokalise; community reviewers; Claude for first drafts"}, {"name": "Performance optimization for scale", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: Performance, with The Midnight Responder", "desc": "Optimize to handle growth in users and data.", "pitfalls": "Premature optimization · Optimizing the wrong things · Breaking things while optimizing", "ai": "Analyze profiles · Suggest strategies · Generate optimized code", "human": "Real bottlenecks · Tradeoffs · Testing at scale", "check": "The before and after numbers exist for the same load test, and the improvement was where the profile said it would be.", "tools": "Vercel Speed Insights; Lighthouse; Supabase query analyzer (pg_stat_statements); k6"}, {"name": "Cost optimization", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Long Game: Accountant, with The Medium for AI costs", "desc": "Reduce infrastructure, operational, and model costs while keeping quality.", "pitfalls": "Cuts that hurt reliability · Not watching costs continuously · Over provisioning just in case · Model bills nobody owns", "ai": "Analyze cost breakdowns · Find opportunities · Model configurations", "human": "Tradeoffs · Context for spending · Vendor negotiations", "check": "Cost per active user is known, tracked monthly, and someone owns the model bill.", "tools": "Vercel and Supabase billing dashboards; Anthropic Console usage; prompt caching; Helicone for model spend"}, {"name": "Organizational features", "new": false, "mode": "human-ai-collaborate", "role": "The Architect: Software Architect, with The Long Game", "desc": "Features required as the organization grows around the product: roles and permissions, admin controls, audit logs, chapters or groups, single sign on, integrations with partner systems.", "pitfalls": "Building without input from the people who will administer it · Underestimating complexity · Ignoring how partners procure and integrate", "ai": "Research requirements · Generate the code · Write compliance documentation", "human": "Understanding the needs · Security decisions · Partner relationships", "check": "An administrator who is not on the build team can onboard a new group, assign roles, and audit an action without help.", "tools": "Supabase row level security and organizations; Clerk or WorkOS for SSO and roles; an audit log table"}, {"name": "Advanced security measures", "new": false, "mode": "human-leads", "role": "The Architect: Security", "desc": "Security controls for higher stakes environments and partners.", "pitfalls": "Security theater · One time implementation · Ignoring usability impact", "ai": "Research best practices · Generate configurations · Analyze logs", "human": "Threat modeling · Architecture decisions · Incident response planning", "check": "The threat model has been revisited since launch, and every new control maps to a threat on it.", "tools": "Cloudflare WAF; Semgrep; Vanta; a repeat penetration test"}, {"name": "Maintainer succession", "new": false, "mode": "human-only", "role": "The Groundskeeper: Engineering Lead, with the Lab Lead", "desc": "Plan for the product to outlive the team that built it. Volunteers move on. The product should not.", "pitfalls": "Knowledge in one person's head · Handoff as a single meeting · No owner for the model bill · No sunset plan", "ai": "Nothing in the core act. Audit the Foundation for gaps.", "human": "Everything", "check": "Someone who has never touched the codebase can, from the Foundation alone, deploy a fix within a day.", "tools": "The Foundation; GitHub CODEOWNERS; a Notion handoff document; 1Password for shared credentials"}]};
const PROB={"northstar": {"title": "The Product North Star", "paras": ["Generic UX heuristics override product specific principles. The interface that should feel like it was made for the exact person it serves ends up feeling like every other interface. Teams ship features that look right and fail their actual user.", "The Product North Star is the binding design constraint that catches the drift. It names who the product is for, what it must do, and what design moves are off limits, with enough specificity that even AI agents reading it can tell when a recommendation violates it.", "A North Star is not a brand statement. It is the lens through which every feature, every screen, and every line of copy is tested before it ships. When technical correctness and the North Star diverge, the North Star wins. That is what makes a product recognizably itself instead of recognizably its category."]}, "council": {"title": "The Council", "paras": ["Most design and development work is critiqued by a single voice. A designer, a founder, a senior engineer, an opinionated stakeholder. That voice has blind spots, and the blind spots become the product.", "The classic failure mode is consensus that feels strong and is wrong: a team agrees on a path that elegantly solves the problem its members can see, and is silent about the problem none of them can see.", "The Council is a structured set of voices, human and AI, each with a different lens on the work. The Council does not vote. It surfaces tensions. Where voices disagree, the disagreement is itself the artifact: a decision the team faces, named clearly, not flattened into a single compromised answer. Where the voices agree, it is strong signal to proceed.", "That structure scales. Any practitioner can learn to operate the Council, which means the discipline of multi perspective critique becomes a repeatable practice instead of a singular act."]}, "foundation": {"title": "The Foundation", "paras": ["Three problems, all of them quiet, all of them compounding.", "<b>The first is tunnel vision.</b> AI agents are powerful when they can see the relevant context and dangerous when they cannot. An agent asked to change a function will confidently change a function, with no awareness of the five places that function is called from, the guarantee that depends on its return shape, or the deployment step that breaks if its signature shifts. Without a roadmap, the agent does precise work in the wrong frame.", "<b>The second is context budget.</b> Even the best agents have finite working memory. Asking an agent to \"understand the codebase\" by reading all of it is wasteful and, as systems grow, impossible. The agent needs to load what is relevant for the task at hand and nothing more, which requires the codebase to be organized as a router, not a library.", "<b>The third is stale documentation.</b> Most architectural documentation rots from the day it is written, because no workflow forces it to stay current. Stale docs are worse than missing docs. They actively mislead the next developer, human or AI, who trusts what they read.", "The Foundation is a living architecture documentation system that addresses all three."]}};
const NS_EXAMPLE={"project": "Larkin", "source": "scan", "scanNote": "Drafted from PRODUCT_NORTH_STAR.md, INDEX.md, and the notification and filtering code. The who and the thesis are quoted from the document; the rest is proposed from them.", "who": "Larkin is built for mothers of young children. Not parents in the abstract. A woman raising young kids, carrying the default parent load: schedules, appointments, permission slips, emotional labor, the mental list of what every family member needs this week. She may have a partner who helps. She may not. Either way, she is the one whose phone gets the school email, whose brain holds the pediatrician's number, whose calendar tracks who needs to be where.", "thesis": "Larkin exists to reduce the invisible load. To be her body double. To see her. To help her see herself again. She is not failing. She is buried under labor that is not seen. Larkin's job is to carry that load with her, so she can put her attention where it belongs: on the people in front of her, and on herself.", "principles": [{"title": "Reduce the invisible load", "body": "Every interaction is a tax on attention she does not have. If a feature asks her to decide, audit, verify, configure, or train, it is the wrong feature, however sound it is technically. The right shape is Larkin handles it; she finds out only if she needs to.", "test": "Does this require her to think about Larkin, or does it let her think about her life?", "by": "artisan", "status": "accepted"}, {"title": "Be her body double", "body": "Larkin acts on her behalf without requiring her to direct each action. When Larkin is uncertain, the answer is rarely to ask her. Make a careful guess and learn quietly. When Larkin gets it wrong, the recovery happens inside Larkin, through better defaults and ambient learning, never by handing the work back as a review screen.", "test": "If Larkin is unsure, does it guess and learn, or does it interrupt her?", "by": "witness", "status": "accepted"}, {"title": "See her", "body": "The product knows who she is, what is on her plate, what is happening in her family this week. Generic, decontextualized output is failure. Specificity is care.", "test": "Is this reporting on data, or speaking to her about her actual life?", "by": "artisan", "status": "accepted"}, {"title": "Help her see herself again", "body": "She has lost track of herself under the weight of invisible labor. Larkin surfaces her own life back to her: what she did, a win she would have missed, a reminder that she is a person and not a logistics engine. This is the most ambitious principle and the easiest to forget.", "test": "Does this acknowledge what she did, or only what is still to do?", "by": "merchant", "status": "proposed"}], "pairs": [{"generic": "3 emails today.", "specific": "Leo's field trip permission slip is due Friday, and Hudson's pediatrician confirmed Tuesday at 2:30.", "note": "Notifications"}, {"generic": "Review 4 items Larkin filtered.", "specific": "She adds the calendar event Larkin missed; next time that sender writes, Larkin catches it. She never sees a queue.", "note": "Filtering"}, {"generic": "Email from school@concord.edu received.", "specific": "The school moved pickup to 2 on Thursday.", "note": "Vocabulary"}, {"generic": "", "specific": "", "note": "Moments that restore her"}], "antipatterns": [{"text": "Audit logs, filtered surfaces, review queues, anything that asks her to verify Larkin's work. The right answer when Larkin is wrong is that Larkin gets smarter.", "from": "Sitting two"}, {"text": "Notifications that demand cognitive work in the middle of her day: decide, approve, configure, verify. Time sensitive information she needs (soccer moved to 7) is the opposite and is welcome.", "from": "Sitting two"}, {"text": "Configuration screens that require her to teach Larkin: toggles, sender whitelists, rule editors.", "from": "Sitting two"}, {"text": "Transparency over trust. Auditable means she can check Larkin's work. Trustworthy means she does not have to.", "from": "Council run council-2026-09-14, tension 2 (Questioner vs the Mother)"}, {"text": "Streak counters, missed email badges, productivity scores, anything that makes her feel more behind. The product's posture is: I see how much you are carrying. Let me help.", "from": "Council run council-2026-09-21, tension 1 (Merchant vs the Mother)"}], "test": ["What does this require her to do? If the verb is review, approve, configure, decide, verify, audit, or teach, look harder. Those verbs are the smell of load being added.", "What does this give her back? Time, attention, a piece of her own life, the feeling of being seen. If the answer is data, options, or control, look harder.", "Would she notice this exists, or would it just quietly work? If a feature wants to announce itself, it is probably wrong.", "If this fails, who carries the failure? If the answer is she does, the design is wrong. Larkin carries its own failures."], "sme": {"name": "The Mother", "seat": "Lived experience", "speaks": "First person, present tense, from the middle of a weekday. She says what she would say to a friend at pickup, and she does not use product words.", "carries": "The default parent load: the school email, the pediatrician's number, who needs to be where, and the list in her head that nobody else can see.", "asks": "What does this make me do? What does it give me back? Would I notice it? If it breaks, is that on me?", "limits": "She speaks only from the who section and the contrast pairs above. Outside those she says she does not know and asks the Witness to find out from real people.", "quotes": ["I keep the whole week in my head and nobody else can see it.", "If one more app wants me to set it up, I will delete it."]}, "critique": {"artisan": {"read": "The thesis is the best sentence in the file. 'Body double' is the one phrase in it that is yours and not hers.", "concerns": [{"section": "thesis", "text": "'Body double' comes from coaching language. Would she say it? If not, keep it in the principles where it is a design idea, and let the thesis stay in her words."}, {"section": "who", "text": "'Young children' ends when? The product behaves differently for a nursing mother and a mother of a nine year old. Say the season."}], "ask": "Replace 'body double' in the thesis with what it does for her, in the words she would use."}, "witness": {"read": "The person is unusually real. The thing she is afraid of is not on the page yet.", "concerns": [{"section": "who", "text": "What is she worried will happen if she drops something? Her own words for that fear belong here, because every notification design will be tested against it."}, {"section": "thesis", "text": "'She is not failing' is a claim about her. Does she believe it? If she does not, the product's first job is different from the one described."}], "ask": "Add one sentence she has actually said, about the moment the load is heaviest."}, "merchant": {"read": "The alternative is her own head plus a shared calendar, and the thesis already beats it. The word that will be tested in the market is 'invisible'.", "concerns": [{"section": "thesis", "text": "'Reduce the invisible load' will be read by her partner too. Does the thesis want him to see it, or is this hers alone? The answer changes what gets built."}, {"section": "who", "text": "'She may have a partner who helps. She may not.' is honest and also the fork in every pricing and sharing decision. Pick a default."}], "ask": "State in one line whether Larkin is hers, or the household's, by default."}}};
